drm/msm/dpu: keep the core clock vote until runtime suspend

When the last CRTC goes inactive, dpu_core_perf_crtc_update() computes a
core clock rate of zero and hands it to dev_pm_opp_set_rate(). That does
not slow the clock down. It disables the OPP table, which drops the MMCX
performance state vote, while the core clock keeps running at its last
rate until the device is runtime suspended. Runtime resume then ungates
the clock at that same rate, and the vote only returns when the first
commit is flushed.

On the Galaxy Tab S8 Ultra the 2960x1848 120 Hz DSC mode needs the top
500 MHz OPP, so every display wake ran the DPU at 500 MHz with MMCX at
its floor between runtime resume and the first flush. The first frame
after each wake failed and the following display-off timed out:

  [dpu error]enc35 frame done timeout
  _dpu_encoder_phys_cmd_wait_for_idle: id:35 pp:0 kickoff timeout 0 cnt 1
    koff_cnt 1
  dpu_encoder_phys_cmd_wait_for_tx_complete: failed wait_for_idle: id:35
    ret:-110 intf:1

After a few off/on cycles a wake reset the SoC with nothing logged.
Pinning the core clock through the core_perf debugfs fixed mode, which
never votes zero, made the cycles clean with the bus votes unchanged.

Skip the zero-rate update. The power domain already drops a device's
performance state at runtime suspend and restores it before runtime
resume, so MMCX still falls while the display is off (performance state
64 off, 256 on) and is back before the clock is ungated.

Generated from the pinned Linux 7.2 source. Not yet submitted upstream.

--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_core_perf.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_core_perf.c
@@ -394,6 +394,17 @@
 
 		trace_dpu_core_perf_update_clk(kms->dev, !crtc->enabled, clk_rate);
 
+		/*
+		 * A rate of zero means that no CRTC is active any more. It does
+		 * not slow the core clock down, it only drops the performance
+		 * state vote, while the clock keeps running at its last rate
+		 * until runtime suspend and is ungated at that rate again on
+		 * runtime resume, ahead of the first commit. Keep the vote; the
+		 * power domain drops and restores it across runtime PM.
+		 */
+		if (!clk_rate)
+			return 0;
+
 		clk_rate = min(clk_rate, kms->perf.max_core_clk_rate);
 		ret = dev_pm_opp_set_rate(&kms->pdev->dev, clk_rate);
 		if (ret) {
